WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.047
TypeScript applications often rely on large dependency trees.

2
00:00:04.047 --> 00:00:12.740
A small package choice can bring transitive dependencies, package scripts, build behavior, maintenance risk, and update obligations.

3
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:25.481
Before adding a package, teams should ask who owns it, whether it is maintained, what it does during install or build, how often it changes, and whether the project already has an acceptable alternative.

4
00:00:25.481 --> 00:00:32.750
Lockfiles and controlled versions are important because dependency resolution affects what code actually runs.

5
00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.148
Transitive dependencies matter too.

6
00:00:35.148 --> 00:00:39.795
A direct dependency may look simple while pulling in many other packages.

7
00:00:39.795 --> 00:00:50.586
Security review should include package scripts, generated artifacts, abandoned packages, vulnerability tracking, and the process for updating dependencies safely.

8
00:00:50.586 --> 00:00:53.809
Build configuration is another security surface.

9
00:00:53.809 --> 00:01:08.947
TypeScript compiler settings, strictness, linting, unused code rules, bundler behavior, source maps, debug flags, environment-specific builds, and package metadata all affect what is shipped and how maintainable the application is.

10
00:01:08.947 --> 00:01:17.416
Build options can accidentally expose internals, include development-only behavior, or hide assumptions that should be reviewed.

11
00:01:17.416 --> 00:01:20.264
Secure defaults and repeatable builds help.

12
00:01:20.264 --> 00:01:27.533
Compiler and lint settings should reinforce safe patterns instead of normalizing unsafe casts or ignored errors.

13
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:31.580
Build profiles should make environment differences explicit.

14
00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:40.873
Dependency and build changes should be reviewed, documented, and tested so security-relevant choices are visible rather than hidden in tooling.

15
00:01:40.873 --> 00:01:44.095
Supply chain hygiene is also an ownership problem.

16
00:01:44.095 --> 00:01:54.363
A team should know who reviews new packages, who responds to dependency alerts, who updates lockfiles, and who decides when a risky dependency should be replaced.

17
00:01:54.363 --> 00:01:59.159
Without clear ownership, dependency risk can quietly become nobody's job.

18
00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:05.679
With clear ownership, updates become planned maintenance rather than surprise work during a release.
